{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["And it says, <b>and you shall not wander after your own heart</b> — to warn against it, that they not stray through it. This is what our Rabbis expounded (Sifrei Shelach 115): \"After your own heart\" — this is heresy; \"which you go astray\" — this is idolatry. That they not speculate heretically or idolatrously from the tekhelet, but rather for you all of it shall be for tzitzit, and you shall see it and remember. And they said: \"After your own eyes\" — this is licentiousness, as in the matter written: \"And I know, and am witness, says YHWH\" (Jeremiah 29:23), and the enlightened will understand. And in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah on the verse \"And the advantage of a land is in everything\" (Ecclesiastes 5:8) — and what is this \"land\"? Hewn from it are the heavens, and it is the throne of the Holy One, blessed be He, and it is a precious stone, and it is the sea of wisdom, and corresponding to it is the tekhelet in the tzitzit garment, as Rabbi Meir said: How does tekhelet differ, etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The midrashic passage beginning with Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah transitions into distinctly kabbalistic-tinged cosmological language; rendered literally without expansion per the way of truth constraint."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:59.085712052Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}